From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 49/49] IEEE 1275 5.3.7.2's device-register words were EMPTY

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

forth/device/other.fs defined rb@ rw@ rl@ rb! rw! rl! with bodies containing no
words at all. Not stubs that abort -- empty. So `addr rb@` consumed nothing and
produced nothing, leaving the ADDRESS where the byte should be, and a register
write stored nothing while leaking two cells.

MEASURED AT THE amd64 PROMPT, which is the only way this was ever going to
surface, since it compiles, links and boots:

    b8000 c@   -> 41       (the byte just written there)
    b8000 rb@  -> b8000    at depth 1
    42 b8002 rb!           left depth 2 having stored nothing

THIS IS NOT A DORMANT PATH. forth/device/table.fs:390-395 binds FCode tokens
0x230-0x235 to exactly these six words, so an FCode driver that reads a device
register receives an address and one that writes a register silently does
nothing AND desynchronises its own stack. The presenting symptom is therefore
not "the register read the wrong value" -- it is a stack shift that surfaces
somewhere else entirely, which is the same shape as patch 34's my-#acells bug
and patch 25's decode-bytes.

The gap was known: forth/device/feval.fs:72 carries a FIXME saying byte-load
"uses c@ rather than rb@ for now", which is a workaround at one call site rather
than a fix.

On a memory-mapped-I/O architecture -- every target in this tree -- device
register access IS memory access at the register's address, which is what
5.3.7.2 describes. A platform needing a barrier or a bus byte-swap would
override these; nothing else in the tree defines them, on any arch.

WHY IT IS SAFE TO CHANGE SHARED CODE HERE: the words had no behaviour to
preserve. Anything calling them was already getting an address or a no-op, so
there is no caller that was relying on the old result -- only callers that were
broken. ppc was rebuilt and its track re-run for the same reason patch 28 was:
OpenBIOS's FCode path actually runs there.

diff --git a/forth/device/other.fs b/forth/device/other.fs
index 1bed9b8..f645620 100644
--- a/forth/device/other.fs
+++ b/forth/device/other.fs
@@ -56,24 +56,38 @@ defer (poke)
 
 
 \ 5.3.7.2 Device-register access
-
-: rb@    ( addr -- byte )
-  ;
-  
-: rw@    ( waddr -- w )
-  ;
-  
-: rl@    ( qaddr -- quad )
-  ;
-  
-: rb!    ( byte addr -- )
-  ;
-  
-: rw!    ( w waddr -- )
-  ;
-  
-: rl!    ( quad qaddr -- )
-  ;
+\
+\ LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). These six were EMPTY -- not stubs that abort, bodies
+\ containing no words at all -- so `addr rb@` consumed nothing and produced
+\ nothing, leaving the ADDRESS where the byte should be. Measured at the amd64
+\ prompt 2026-08-29, which is the only way this was ever going to surface:
+\
+\     b8000 c@   -> 41       (the byte just written there)
+\     b8000 rb@  -> b8000    at depth 1
+\     42 b8002 rb!           left depth 2 having stored nothing
+\
+\ THIS IS NOT A DORMANT PATH. forth/device/table.fs:390-395 binds FCode tokens
+\ 0x230-0x235 to exactly these words, so an FCode driver that READS a device
+\ register receives an address, and one that WRITES a register silently does
+\ nothing and leaves two items on the stack -- which desynchronises every
+\ operation after it in that driver. The failure is therefore not "the register
+\ read the wrong value"; it is a stack shift, which surfaces somewhere else
+\ entirely. forth/device/feval.fs:72 already carries a FIXME saying byte-load
+\ "uses c@ rather than rb@ for now", so the gap was known and worked around at
+\ one call site rather than closed.
+\
+\ On a memory-mapped-I/O architecture -- which is every target in this tree --
+\ device-register access IS memory access at the register's address, and that is
+\ what IEEE 1275 5.3.7.2 describes. A platform needing a barrier or a bus
+\ byte-swap would override these; none in this tree does, and nothing else in
+\ the tree defines them.
+
+: rb@    ( addr -- byte )   c@ ;
+: rw@    ( waddr -- w )     w@ ;
+: rl@    ( qaddr -- quad )  l@ ;
+: rb!    ( byte addr -- )   c! ;
+: rw!    ( w waddr -- )     w! ;
+: rl!    ( quad qaddr -- )  l! ;
 
 : rx@ ( oaddr - o )
   state @ if
